5. Develop different strategies for the United States and Europe

The United States and Europe should not be treated as single, homogeneous societies.

European countries differ considerably in their migration histories, ethnic composition, citizenship systems, religious traditions, and definitions of racial identity. The United States has its own distinct history of slavery, segregation, immigration, and racial classification.

Consequently, a successful strategy would require country-specific research.

United States

I would prioritize five areas.

Policy area	Proposed intervention	Intended outcome
Housing	Expand mixed-income housing and housing mobility	More integrated residential environments
Education	Support integrated schools and universities	Broader friendship networks
Technology	Develop voluntary, compatibility-based cross-community discovery	More opportunities to meet compatible partners
Employment	Reduce discrimination and expand professional networks	Greater social and economic integration
Community life	Fund inclusive civic, cultural, and recreational activities	Stronger relationships across communities

American policy should also account for differences among Black, White, Asian, Indigenous, Latino, and multiracial populations.

These categories are not mutually exclusive in every statistical system, and Latino identity, for example, can overlap with multiple racial identities.

Research should reflect that complexity.

Europe

European policy should be adapted to national conditions.

Policy area	Proposed intervention	Intended outcome
Housing	Address residential segregation and housing discrimination	More opportunities for social contact
Education	Support inclusive schools and universities	Cross-community friendships
Employment	Enforce equal-opportunity protections	Fair access to workplaces and professional networks
Language	Provide accessible language education where needed	Easier communication across communities
Community life	Support intercultural activities and local organizations	Greater familiarity and trust
Technology	Encourage privacy-preserving, inclusive dating services	Broader opportunities to meet compatible partners

For example, the United Kingdom, France, Germany, Sweden, and the Netherlands would require different approaches because their migration histories, institutions, and social circumstances differ.

The strategy should also distinguish racial integration from integration across national, linguistic, and religious backgrounds. These dimensions overlap, but they are not identical.

