9. A practical 10-year implementation plan

Here is how I would organize the initiative.

Period	Priority	Main activities
Years 1–2	Research	Establish baselines, examine demographic data, interview couples, and identify barriers
Years 2–3	Pilot programs	Launch intercultural events and voluntary social-integration experiments
Years 3–5	Evaluation	Measure friendship formation, participation, trust, and program effectiveness
Years 4–6	Technology	Test inclusive social-discovery and dating products
Years 5–8	Expansion	Scale successful programs through universities, municipalities, and nonprofits
Years 8–10	Long-term assessment	Evaluate relationship outcomes, discrimination, privacy, and social welfare

The initiative should begin in a small number of willing communities, with appropriate comparison groups and independent researchers.

Programs should expand only when the evidence indicates that they deliver meaningful benefits.

Interracial marriage rates would be a long-term descriptive indicator, not a short-term performance target for individual institutions.

