My final recommendation

If I were designing this initiative from scratch, I would combine three strategies:

First, build integrated social environments. Invest in housing, education, transportation, workplaces, and community spaces that enable people from different backgrounds to interact naturally.

Second, build technology that expands romantic opportunities. Create voluntary dating and social-discovery tools that help compatible people meet outside their existing networks without manipulating their preferences.

Third, use mathematical futurology to evaluate results. Collect appropriate demographic data, test interventions, model long-term scenarios, and expand only the approaches that demonstrate real benefits.

The central hypothesis is that interracial marriage becomes more accessible when people have more opportunities to form meaningful relationships across racial boundaries and face fewer barriers when they do.

The ultimate objective should not be to dictate whom people marry. It should be to build a society in which race is less likely to determine whom people have the opportunity to meet, love, and marry.
